const __pageData = JSON.parse('{"title":"10. Product, sustainability and safety requirements","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"10-product-sustainability-and-safety.md","filePath":"10-product-sustainability-and-safety.md"}');
const _sfc_main = { name: "10-product-sustainability-and-safety.md" };
function _sfc_render(_ctx, _cache, $props, $setup, $data, $options) {
  return openBlock(), createElementBlock("div", null, [..._cache[0] || (_cache[0] = [
    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_10-product-sustainability-and-safety-requirements" tabindex="-1">10. Product, sustainability and safety requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-product-sustainability-and-safety-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Product, sustainability and safety requirements&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_10-1-how-to-use-this-section" tabindex="-1">10.1 How to use this section <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-1-how-to-use-this-section" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.1 How to use this section&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This section summarises the sustainability, safety, labelling and information requirements that apply when batteries are placed on the market or put into service under the Regulation. A battery may be placed on the market or put into service only if it meets those applicable requirements and does not present a risk to human health, the safety of persons, property or the environment.</p><p>The outcome depends on category, capacity, rechargeability, chemistry, configuration, date and, in some cases, prior circular operations.</p><h2 id="_10-2-restrictions-on-substances" tabindex="-1">10.2 Restrictions on substances <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-2-restrictions-on-substances" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.2 Restrictions on substances&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Batteries must not contain substances restricted by Annex I unless the relevant conditions are met. This applies across battery categories and operates in addition to Annex XVII to Regulation (EC) No 1907/2006 and Article 4(2)(a) of Directive 2000/53/EC. The current Annex I must be checked for the substance, battery and use concerned; a general assertion of “substance compliance” is not a substitute (Article 6).</p><h2 id="_10-3-carbon-footprint-requirements" tabindex="-1">10.3 Carbon-footprint requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-3-carbon-footprint-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.3 Carbon-footprint requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Carbon-footprint requirements apply to EV batteries, LMT batteries and rechargeable industrial batteries above 2 kWh. They do not currently apply to portable batteries, SLI batteries or industrial batteries at or below 2 kWh. Rechargeable industrial batteries with exclusively external storage have later dates (Article 7).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Explanation</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>Draw up a declaration for each battery model per manufacturing plant with the Article 7(1) information; it accompanies the battery until accessible through the Article 13(6) QR code</td><td>Article 7(1); Annex II</td></tr><tr><td>Class and label</td><td>Label the battery with its carbon footprint and performance class and demonstrate the calculation and classification in technical documentation</td><td>Article 7(2); Annex II, point 8</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum threshold</td><td>Demonstrate that the declared life-cycle value for the model per plant is below the applicable maximum</td><td>Article 7(3); Annex II, point 9</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each requirement depends on delegated or implementing acts. A base date alone is insufficient: the duty starts on that date or after the specified period following entry into force of the relevant act, whichever is latest. Current methods, formats, classes and thresholds must be verified.</p><p>The legal granularity is model per manufacturing plant, not automatically individual instance. Batch or serial data support traceability. The carbon-footprint requirements do not apply where a battery was already placed on the market or put into service before preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing. This does not cover a newly manufactured battery or one first placed on the market only after the operation (Article 7(5)).</p><h2 id="_10-4-recycled-content-requirements" tabindex="-1">10.4 Recycled-content requirements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-4-recycled-content-requirements" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.4 Recycled-content requirements&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Recycled-content requirements apply to industrial batteries above 2 kWh, EV batteries, LMT batteries and SLI batteries where cobalt, lead, lithium or nickel is present within their material scope. Industrial batteries with exclusively external storage are excluded from the documentation and minimum-share requirement. The duty is conditional on category and material content (Article 8(1)–(3)).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Explanation and legal granularity</th><th>Main base-Regulation date</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Document recovered shares for each battery model per year and per manufacturing plant</td><td>Conditional from 18 August 2028 for industrial, EV and SLI; 18 August 2033 for LMT</td><td>Article 8(1)</td></tr><tr><td>First minimum shares</td><td>Demonstrate 16% cobalt, 85% lead, 6% lithium and 6% nickel in technical documentation</td><td>18 August 2031 for industrial, EV and SLI</td><td>Article 8(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Later minimum shares</td><td>Demonstrate 26% cobalt, 85% lead, 12% lithium and 15% nickel</td><td>18 August 2036, including LMT</td><td>Article 8(3)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first documentation date is 18 August 2028 or 24 months after entry into force of the methodology-and-format delegated act, whichever is latest. Targets, materials and shares may be amended or supplemented, so current law must be checked (Article 8(5)–(6)).</p><p>Article 8 sets model–year–plant granularity. The model, year and plant information must be retained. Instance access should resolve to the correct model–year–plant record. A battery with a battery passport has an individual identifier while that does not make an individual recycled-content calculation necessary. This does not apply to batteries that have been subject to circular-operations such as preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing. if the batteries had already been placed on the market or put into service before undergoing such operations.</p><h2 id="_10-5-performance-and-durability" tabindex="-1">10.5 Performance and durability <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-5-performance-and-durability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.5 Performance and durability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Regulation establishes distinct category-specific performance and durability requirements (Articles 9–10).</p><h3 id="_10-5-1-portable-batteries-of-general-use" tabindex="-1">10.5.1 Portable batteries of general use <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-5-1-portable-batteries-of-general-use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.5.1 Portable batteries of general use&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Portable batteries of general use, excluding button cells, must meet minimum values for the Annex III parameters. This applies from 18 August 2028 or 24 months after the relevant delegated act enters into force, whichever is latest. Until that act is verified, mandatory values and the operative date cannot be supplied.</p><h3 id="_10-5-2-rechargeable-industrial-lmt-and-ev-batteries" tabindex="-1">10.5.2 Rechargeable industrial, LMT and EV batteries <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-5-2-rechargeable-industrial-lmt-and-ev-batteries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.5.2 Rechargeable industrial, LMT and EV batteries&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Explanation and scope</th><th>Main base-Regulation date</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Performance and durability document</td><td>Rechargeable industrial batteries above 2 kWh, LMT batteries and EV batteries must be accompanied by a document containing the applicable Annex IV, performance and durability values. The technical documentation must explain the specifications, standards and conditions used to measure, calculate or estimate those values and contain at least the Annex IV, information.</td><td>18 August 2024</td><td>Article 10(1); Annex IV; Annex VIII</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum values for rechargeable industrial batteries</td><td>Rechargeable industrial batteries above 2 kWh must meet minimum values established for the applicable Annex IV, performance and durability parameters. Batteries with exclusively external storage are excluded.</td><td>Conditional from 18 August 2027 or 18 months after the relevant delegated act enters into force, whichever is later</td><td>Article 10(2) and (5)</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum values for LMT batteries</td><td>LMT batteries must meet minimum values established for the applicable Annex IV, performance and durability parameters.</td><td>Conditional from 18 August 2028 or 18 months after the relevant delegated act enters into force, whichever is later</td><td>Article 10(3) and (5)</td></tr><tr><td>EV battery minimum values</td><td>EV batteries are subject to the document and technical-documentation requirements, but Article 10 does not establish a subsequent minimum-value requirement for them.</td><td>No subsequent minimum-value date under Article 10</td><td>Article 10</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The requirements do not apply after preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing where the operator demonstrates that the battery had already been placed on the market or put into service before the relevant requirement became applicable.</p><p>Values must belong to the battery, not be incorrectly attributed to a host appliance or vehicle. Identify both objects and their relationship.</p><h2 id="_10-6-removability-and-replaceability" tabindex="-1">10.6 Removability and replaceability <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-6-removability-and-replaceability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.6 Removability and replaceability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From 18 February 2027, the responsible actor is any natural or legal person placing on the market a product incorporating the battery; this is not only a battery-manufacturer duty (Articles 11 and 96(2)(a)).</p><p>For products incorporating portable batteries, the entire battery must be readily removable and replaceable by the end-user throughout the product lifetime. For end-user removability and replaceability duty, the obligation applies only to the entire portable battery, not to individual cells or other parts contained within it. Commercially available tools are permitted, but not specialised tools unless supplied free, proprietary tools, thermal energy or solvents. The product must have instructions and safety information on battery use, removal and replacement, also permanently available online on a public website in an easily understandable form.</p><p>The qualifications and related duties must be preserved:</p><ul><li>professional-only removal is permitted for specified water-exposed appliances where required for user and appliance safety;</li><li>specified medical imaging, radiotherapy and in-vitro diagnostic devices are covered by a further derogation;</li><li>the end-user removability and replaceability requirement does not apply where a permanent connection is necessary for safety or, for products whose main function is collecting and supplying data, data integrity;</li><li>products incorporating LMT batteries must permit the battery and individual cells in its pack to be removed and replaced by an independent professional;</li><li>a replacement must be compatible without affecting functioning, performance or safety;</li><li>portable and LMT batteries must remain available as spare parts at a reasonable and non-discriminatory price for at least five years after the last unit of the equipment model is placed on the market; and</li><li>software must not impede replacement with a compatible battery or key components.</li></ul><h2 id="_10-7-safety-of-stationary-battery-energy-storage-systems" tabindex="-1">10.7 Safety of stationary battery energy storage systems <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-7-safety-of-stationary-battery-energy-storage-systems" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.7 Safety of stationary battery energy storage systems&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The safety requirements apply irrespective of whether system capacity exceeds the battery-passport threshold. (Articles 12 and 77(1)).</p><p>Stationary battery energy storage systems must be safe during normal operation and use. From 18 August 2024, technical documentation must demonstrate compliance and successful testing for applicable parameters using state-of-the-art methods (Annex V); assess hazards not addressed by Annex V; evidence successful mitigation and testing; and include mitigation instructions for hazards that could occur, such as fire or explosion. A parameter applies only where the corresponding hazard exists under conditions envisaged by the manufacturer. Documentation must be reviewed after preparation for re-use, preparation for repurposing, repurposing or remanufacturing.</p><h2 id="_10-8-state-of-health-and-expected-lifetime" tabindex="-1">10.8 State of health and expected lifetime <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-8-state-of-health-and-expected-lifetime" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.8 State of health and expected lifetime&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For stationary battery energy storage systems, LMT batteries and EV batteries, battery management systems must contain up-to-date parameter data from 18 August 2024 as stated in Annex VII. This does not extend to every industrial battery above 2 kWh (Article 14).</p><p>Read-only access must be provided, respecting manufacturer intellectual property rights and on a non-discriminatory basis, to the person who legally purchased the battery—including an independent operator or waste management operator—or a third party acting for that person. Access is limited to energy-storage participation; evaluation of residual value, remaining lifetime or further-use capability; and facilitation of listed circular operations.</p><p>The system must include a software reset function where a circular operator needs different software. If used, the original manufacturer is not liable for a breach attributable to software uploaded after placement on the market. For EV batteries, these requirements apply in addition to Union vehicle type-approval law.</p><h2 id="_10-9-category-applicability-matrix" tabindex="-1">10.9 Category applicability matrix <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-9-category-applicability-matrix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.9 Category applicability matrix&quot;">​</a></h2><p>“Conditional” means subject to the stated capacity, material, rechargeability, configuration, date or secondary act.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Portable / general use</th><th>LMT</th><th>SLI</th><th>Industrial</th><th>EV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Restrictions on substances Article 6</td><td>Applies</td><td>Applies</td><td>Applies</td><td>Applies</td><td>Applies</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon footprint Article 7</td><td>No current duty</td><td>Conditional stages</td><td>No current duty</td><td>Rechargeable above 2 kWh; later external-storage dates</td><td>Conditional stages</td></tr><tr><td>Recycled content Article 8</td><td>No current duty</td><td>Conditional</td><td>Conditional</td><td>Above 2 kWh, excluding exclusively external storage; conditional</td><td>Conditional</td></tr><tr><td>Performance Article 9</td><td>General-use, excluding button cells; conditional</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Performance Article 10</td><td>No</td><td>Applies</td><td>No</td><td>Rechargeable above 2 kWh</td><td>Applies</td></tr><tr><td>Later minimums Article 10</td><td>No</td><td>Conditional</td><td>No</td><td>Rechargeable above 2 kWh, excluding exclusively external storage; conditional</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Removal/replacement Article 11</td><td>When incorporated; Article 11(2)–(3) apply</td><td>Professional removal when incorporated</td><td>Only if also portable; classify first</td><td>Only if also within an Article 11 category; classify first</td><td>No duty as EV</td></tr><tr><td>Storage safety Article 12</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>When a stationary storage system</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>State of health Article 14</td><td>No</td><td>Applies</td><td>No</td><td>Only when a stationary storage system</td><td>Applies</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Category overlap must not be assumed. See Section 4, “Determining the battery category”.</p><h2 id="_10-10-application-dates-and-dependencies" tabindex="-1">10.10 Application dates and dependencies <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-10-application-dates-and-dependencies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.10 Application dates and dependencies&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Date or condition</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Reference/dependency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18 February 2024</td><td>Article 5 and 6 framework</td><td>Articles 5–6 and 96(1), subject to transitions/current Annex I</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2024</td><td>Performance document; storage-safety documentation; state-of-health data/access</td><td>Articles 10(1), 12(2) and 14</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2025*</td><td>EV carbon declaration</td><td>Article 7(1)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2026*</td><td>Industrial carbon declaration, except external storage</td><td>Article 7(1)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2026*</td><td>EV carbon class/label</td><td>Article 7(2)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>18 February 2027</td><td>Removability and replaceability</td><td>Articles 11 and 96(2)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2027*</td><td>Industrial performance minimums and carbon class/label, except external storage</td><td>Articles 10(2) and 7(2)(b); separate secondary acts apply</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2028*</td><td>EV maximum carbon threshold</td><td>Article 7(3)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2028*</td><td>LMT carbon declaration; Article 8 documentation for industrial/EV/SLI; Article 9 and LMT Article 10 minimums</td><td>Articles 7(1)(c), 8(1), 9 and 10(3); separate secondary acts apply</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2029*</td><td>Industrial maximum carbon threshold, except external storage</td><td>Article 7(3)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2030*</td><td>LMT carbon class/label</td><td>Article 7(2)(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2030*</td><td>External-storage industrial carbon declaration</td><td>Article 7(1)(d)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2031, subject to amendment</td><td>First recovered-content minimums</td><td>Article 8(2), subject to Article 8(5)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2031*</td><td>LMT maximum carbon threshold</td><td>Article 7(3)(c)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 February 2032*</td><td>External-storage industrial carbon class/label</td><td>Article 7(2)(d)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2033</td><td>LMT recovered-content documentation</td><td>Article 8(1)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional from 18 August 2033*</td><td>External-storage industrial maximum carbon threshold</td><td>Article 7(3)(d)</td></tr><tr><td>18 August 2036, subject to amendment</td><td>Later recovered-content minimums, including LMT</td><td>Article 8(3), subject to Article 8(5)–(6)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>* A conditional requirement applies from the date shown or after the period specified in the Regulation following the entry into force of the relevant delegated or implementing act, whichever is later. The applicable secondary act and waiting period differ by requirement and battery category. Before relying on a conditional date, verify the relevant act, its entry-into-force date and the period specified in the cited provision.</p><p>The base Regulation does not itself provide every methodology, format, performance class, threshold or minimum value needed to apply these requirements. Some details depend on delegated or implementing acts, which must be obtained and verified before implementation.</p><p>Open matters include current Article 11 guidance and derogations (OQ-REG-004), capacity evidence at the 2 kWh stationary-storage passport boundary (OQ-REG-005), SLI QR recycled-content content before Article 8(1) applies (OQ-REG-006), verified GDSN coverage (OQ-GS1-001) and GS1 Digital Link resolver governance (OQ-GS1-003). They prevent a claim that all implementation detail is complete. See <code>research/open-questions.md</code> and <code>research/source-gaps.md</code>.</p><h2 id="_10-11-consolidated-gs1-support-and-granularity" tabindex="-1">10.11 Consolidated GS1 support and granularity <a class="header-anchor" href="#_10-11-consolidated-gs1-support-and-granularity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10.11 Consolidated GS1 support and granularity&quot;">​</a></h2><p>GS1 standards can support companies in implementing the Regulation by providing a consistent way to identify batteries, economic operators, production plants, and related entities, as well as to capture and share relevant data.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Requirement area</th><th>How GS1 standards may support the function</th><th>Granularity and limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Restrictions on substances</td><td>A GTIN identifies the trade-item model associated with a controlled material declaration. GTIN plus batch or lot number associates production evidence with a batch, while GTIN plus serial number supports an instance investigation. A GLN identifies a manufacturer, testing function or manufacturing location. EPCIS may share material-receipt, production, release, recall and corrective-action events.</td><td>Use model identification for an unchanged specification and batch identification where inputs or test results vary. These standards do not validate composition or establish conformity. A GLN does not replace a legally required identity, accreditation or authority-issued identifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Carbon footprint</td><td>A GTIN combined with a batch or lot number may provide access to the carbon footprint declaration provided there is a link between that batch or lot to a single manufacturing plant. A GLN can be used to identify the manufacturing plant to enable the link to the manufacturing plant.EPCIS can be used to share data about carbon footprint.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>GS1 Digital Link encoded in a QR can help parties in the supply chain to access relevant information.</td><td>Although the regulation does not explicitly require a batch number, it requires that the data associated with the record must distinguish between the manufacturing plant. For this reason, using a GTIN together with a batch/lot number is recommended. This approach helps ensure that the relevant carbon footprint record can be clearly linked to the specific battery model, manufacturing plant and production batch.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Recycled content</td><td>At a minimum, a GTIN combined with a batch/lot number enables companies to document and track levels of recycled content as long as the batch is made up of same amount of recycled content. Where instance level identification is applied for example when there is a requirement for a battery passport, a GTIN and Serial number can be used. GTIN and batch/lot to identify the content used. A GLN can be used to identify the manufacturing plant to enable the link to the manufacturing plant. EPCIS can be used to share data about recycled content. GS1 Digital Link encoded in a QR code can help parties in the supply chain to access relevant information such as model, year and plant.</td><td>The Regulation requires companies to track, document, and eventually meet minimum levels of recycled cobalt, lead, lithium, and nickel in the battery types in scope. Since the information must be specific enough to distinguish between battery models, production years, and manufacturing plants, it is recommended to use at least a GTIN together with a batch/lot number. For batteries that require a product passport and instance-level identification, a GTIN together with a serial number can also be used to access the relevant information for the specific model–year–plant record. Identification of raw materials makes it easier to keep track of the recycled content.</td></tr><tr><td>Performance and durability</td><td>A GTIN identifies the model to which a performance document applies. Global product master data enables the documentation and sharing of performance and durability information. GS1 Web Vocabulary has definitions for corresponding data points required for performance and durability. A GS1 Digital Link URI may enable access to the battery performance and durability information</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>The regulation requires performance and durability information such as how long the battery lasts, how well it performs after storage, how many times it can be recharged, where relevant, and whether it is resistant to leakage. As this information should be common at a model level A GTIN is sufficient. Sharing the master data with GDSN enables other parties to access the data.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>GS1 Web Vocabulary supports the possibility to retrieve structured, machine-readable master data.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Removability and replaceability</td><td>GTIN to identify the model. A separate GTIN to identify the host product. A GS1 Digital Link URI may enable access to instructions and safety information on the use, removal and replacement of the batteries and a GLN identifies a party or service location. A GTIN plus serial number could be used to keep track of replacement and repair of batteries.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A battery GTIN identifies the battery trade item but does not, by itself, identify the host model in which the battery is incorporated or record when the last unit of that host model was placed on the market. The manufacturer must maintain the required removal and replacement instructions for the applicable legal period. The instruction resource may therefore need to remain accessible after the ordinary commercial product page has been withdrawn.</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Safety of stationary battery energy storage systems</td><td>Master data relating to the model can be used to share information about safert information and instructions. A GS1 Digital Link URI may enable access to instructions and safety information on the use, removal and replacement of the batteries. A GTIN plus serial number could be used to enable parties to review if a battery is prepared for re-use, prepared for repurposing, remanufactured or repurposed. EPCIS can share commissioning, inspection, maintenance or circular-operation events.</td><td>Identification at model level is sufficient to provide information about the safety instructions. As the battery category requires a battery passport and hence instance level identification, it will have a GTIN plus serial number and this information can be used to provide technical documentation at instance level to make it possible to review information if a battery is prepared for re-use, prepared for repurposing, remanufactured or repurposed.</td></tr><tr><td>State of health and expected lifetime</td><td>A GTIN can be used to document and access information about safety, tests and instructions for a battery model. However, a GTIN plus serial number will make it possible to track and provide state of the health information if the battery is re-used, prepared for repurposing, remanufactured or repurposed. A GS1 Digital Link URI may provide an entry point to access-controlled resources, and EPCIS may support lifecycle events.</td><td>Identification at model level is sufficient to provide generic information about the battery safety tests, instructions. Because condition changes over time, instance identification is normally required; model master data are insufficient. Battery manufacturers must ensure that certain battery data related to state of health and expected lifetime—can be accessed through the battery management system on a read-only basis. This access must be provided fairly and without discrimination to the legal battery owner, independent operators, waste management operators, or authorised third parties, while still respecting the manufacturer’s intellectual property rights. Verifiable credentials structured using the GS1 Verifiable Credentials Data Model may support machine-verifiable assertions about a party’s identity, role or delegated authority. An access-control system may use those assertions when deciding whether access should be granted. Verifiable credentials do not themselves enforce access rights, and implementation depends on credential issuance, trust, revocation, authentication and authorisation governance.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>See Section 12, “Battery and grouping identification”, for identifier allocation and granularity, and Section 19, “Supply-chain traceability and information exchange”, for data-sharing implementation.</p>', 49)
  ])]);
}
const _10ProductSustainabilityAndSafety = /* @__PURE__ */ _export_sfc(_sfc_main, [["render", _sfc_render]]);
